[orch:err] ## PR description (untrusted)
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] ## Summary
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] Fixes #191395
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] Fix for UnboundLocalError in Elastic etcd find_free_port when socket creation fails.
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] ## Root Cause
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] The `find_free_port` function in `torch/distributed/elastic/rendezvous/etcd_server.py` had a bug where the variable `s` (socket) was not initialized before the try block. When `socket.socket()` failed on the first attempt with an `OSError`, the code tried to call `s.close()` in the exception handler, but `s` was still undefined, causing an `UnboundLocalError`. This masked the underlying socket creation failure and made debugging difficult.
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] ## Fix
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] The fix initializes the socket variable `s` to `None` before the try block and adds a check in the exception handler to only close the socket if it was successfully created. Additionally, added an explicit `continue` statement to ensure the function tries the next address. This allows socket creation to fail gracefully and continue to the next address, preventing the UnboundLocalError and properly exposing the socket creation failure.
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] ## Testing
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] - Added regression test `test_find_free_port_handles_socket_creation_failure` in `test/distributed/elastic/rendezvous/etcd_server_test.py`
[orch:err] - Verified original reproduction script from issue now runs cleanly
[orch:err] - Confirmed existing related tests still pass
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] ## Notes for Reviewers
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] - Tested on ARM64 CPU-only (Oracle Cloud Ampere A1 Flex, no GPU)
[orch:err] - Python 3.11.9 | PyTorch 2.4.0
[orch:err] - The fix is minimal and follows PyTorch's existing code patterns
[orch:err] - The regression test covers the exact scenario described in the issue
[orch:err] 
