[orch:err] ### pytorch/pytorch#191395 — [Elastic] etcd find_free_port masks socket creation failures
[orch:err] - State: `OPEN` · Closing-link from PR: yes · Source: `closing_hash`
[orch:err] - URL: https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/issues/191395
[orch:err] - Author: d4l3k
[orch:err] - Labels: good first issue, triaged, module: elastic, oncall: distributed infra
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] #### Issue body
[orch:err] ### Problem
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] On current `main` (`5d242823c6de2bbc21d636b2995a38340bd2a16c`), `torch.distributed.elastic.rendezvous.etcd_server.find_free_port()` assigns `s` inside a `try` block but unconditionally calls `s.close()` from the `except OSError` block.
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] Relevant code: [etcd_server.py](https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/5d242823c6de2bbc21d636b2995a38340bd2a16c/torch/distributed/elastic/rendezvous/etcd_server.py#L50-L64).
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] If `socket.socket()` itself raises on the first address, `s` was never assigned and cleanup raises `UnboundLocalError`, masking the actual socket error.
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] ### Reproduction
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] ```python
[orch:err] from unittest.mock import patch
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] from torch.distributed.elastic.rendezvous.etcd_server import find_free_port
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] with patch("socket.getaddrinfo", return_value=[(2, 1, 6, "", ("127.0.0.1", 0))]), \
[orch:err]      patch("socket.socket", side_effect=OSError("socket failed")):
[orch:err]     find_free_port()
[orch:err] ```
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] The function raises `UnboundLocalError` from `s.close()` instead of trying remaining addresses or ending with its documented `RuntimeError`.
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] ### Expected behavior
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] Only close a socket that was successfully created. Socket construction/bind/listen errors should retain their useful diagnostics, and the function should continue through available addresses before raising `RuntimeError`.
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] ### Impact
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] Failures caused by resource exhaustion, unsupported address families, or platform socket errors are replaced by a misleading local-variable error.
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] ### Suggested regression test
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] Mock the first socket constructor to raise and provide a subsequent usable address; assert the second address is attempted. Also test all attempts failing and assert no `UnboundLocalError` escapes.
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] cc @dzhulgakov @awgu @wanchaol @fegin @fduwjj @wz337 @wconstab @pragupta @msaroufim @dcci @aditvenk @weifengpy @kapilsh
[orch:err] 
[orch:err] #### Comments (last 1 of 1)
[orch:err] - **@Neeraj8180:** Hi, I'd like to work on this. Plan: initialize the socket variable before the try/except in `find_free_port()` so we only call `s.close()` when a socket was actually created, preserving the original `OSError` diagnostics and continuing through the remaining addresses before raising `RuntimeError`. I'll add regression tests that mock socket creation failing on the first address (and on all addresses) to assert no `UnboundLocalError` escapes. Could this be assigned to me?
[orch:err] 
